  <div class="attributed-text-segment-list__container relative mt-1 mb-1.5 babybear:mt-0 babybear:mb-0.5">
    <p class="attributed-text-segment-list__content text-color-text !text-sm whitespace-pre-wrap break-words
        " dir="ltr" data-test-id="main-feed-activity-card__commentary">With NIS2 now in force, many operators are assessing what a cyber security risk assessment actually means in an OT context. The challenge is rarely the requirement itself, but to integrate the cyber security work with other areas within risk management. Operations teams may be left out of the conversation, and the link to process consequences and SIS documentation can be lost. Without integration and alignment with other disciplines, the result is a report that satisfies a checkbox without providing much value. 
 
In our experience, effective CSRAs in industrial environments benefit from a cross-disciplinary approach from the start: 
• Integration with existing PHA studies (HAZOP/LOPA)
• Involvement from operations and engineering disciplines 
• Structured methodology grounded in IEC 62443 and aligned with IEC 61511.
 
The output should give operators a clearer picture of how cyber events can propagate into process consequences, and whether existing safeguards are adequate. This requires understanding the process, the control system, and the safety functions. 
 
Read more about integrating cyber security in process safety management here: <a class="link" href="https://lnkd.in/emGAdJJ3?trk=organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" target="_self" rel="nofollow" data-tracking-control-name="organization_guest_main-feed-card-text" data-tracking-will-navigate>https://lnkd.in/emGAdJJ3</a></p>
<!---->  </div>



        

        
            
      
    
    
    

